[{:db/id 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c3d7a176-49a0-5f40-a976-fffa17513013" "question-08c8aba3-84ff-578f-a738-778ea74bd824" "question-60075bc6-3e97-52cb-9240-f8444fe4b019"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124432 :question/problem "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $I_2 \\in M$\n\nII. For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](images/05/05984749a7ebbc4315a775cc1dd628030c440891e9e902779f1a472b3be349cf.svg) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](images/bb/bb4cbc16280f0fe489b5842999c145f913ab9776a9abb12d228bf189ba0eea4c.svg) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](images/d6/d67cdce6dcf1167f849806639ff28e1de394dd9d81c0c9a9cb0a17e33a0da1c6.svg)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124297 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L,$ we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x).$\n\nII. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$\n\nIII. For each $p(x) \\in L,$ we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124400 :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0.$ Which of the following properties are true?\n\nI. For any $A \\in P,$ we have $1 \\cdot A = A.$\n\nII. For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.$\n\nIII. For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278339 :question/problem "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n\nII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under addition." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c3d7a176-49a0-5f40-a976-fffa17513013" :question/id #uuid "c3d7a176-49a0-5f40-a976-fffa17513013" :question/math-academy-id "q-141158" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{11}{a}_{21}+{a}_{12}{a}_{22}>0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}$ belongs to $T.$\n\nII. For every $A\\in T$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha \\cdot A\\in T.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{11}{a}_{21}+{a}_{12}{a}_{22}>0.$ However, for the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have ${a}_{11}{a}_{21}+{a}_{12}{a}_{22}=1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1=0≯0.$ So, ${I}_{2}\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, let $\\alpha =0\\in R$ and $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in T.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\alpha \\cdot A=0\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere the sum of the products of columns' entries is $0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$ Hence, $0\\cdot A\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5e550c1-c444-5056-9fdd-7c27dc6bda79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5e550c1-c444-5056-9fdd-7c27dc6bda79" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e550c1-c444-5056-9fdd-7c27dc6bda79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54c85eef-42f1-5a34-b835-c200174ad259" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25b7f412-8325-5a8d-8ec0-efdd1fcf99b4" "answer-957c96fc-b399-5084-82f0-23bab95940c6" "answer-9f8780bf-4b01-52e4-a8c3-5d2fd02f1d5f" "answer-ae42fca6-e22f-5cdd-ab0e-b0f6c6d5db30" "answer-54c85eef-42f1-5a34-b835-c200174ad259"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25b7f412-8325-5a8d-8ec0-efdd1fcf99b4" :answer/id #uuid "25b7f412-8325-5a8d-8ec0-efdd1fcf99b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-957c96fc-b399-5084-82f0-23bab95940c6" :answer/id #uuid "957c96fc-b399-5084-82f0-23bab95940c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f8780bf-4b01-52e4-a8c3-5d2fd02f1d5f" :answer/id #uuid "9f8780bf-4b01-52e4-a8c3-5d2fd02f1d5f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae42fca6-e22f-5cdd-ab0e-b0f6c6d5db30" :answer/id #uuid "ae42fca6-e22f-5cdd-ab0e-b0f6c6d5db30" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54c85eef-42f1-5a34-b835-c200174ad259" :answer/id #uuid "54c85eef-42f1-5a34-b835-c200174ad259" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-08c8aba3-84ff-578f-a738-778ea74bd824" :question/id #uuid "08c8aba3-84ff-578f-a738-778ea74bd824" :question/math-academy-id "q-94691" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $r(x)=|x|,$ then $r(x)\\in D.$\n\nII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in D,$ we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in D.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $r(x)=|x|\\in D$, because\n\n$$\nr(0)=|0|=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x),g(x)\\in D,$ where $f(0)=0$ and $g(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=0+0=0.\n$$\n\nHence $f(x)+g(x)\\in D.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II it true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62c40c83-2c46-579c-a4e6-29aa8f8ba721"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62c40c83-2c46-579c-a4e6-29aa8f8ba721" :answer-field/id #uuid "62c40c83-2c46-579c-a4e6-29aa8f8ba721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d7548b7c-b486-525d-8943-136fc6cfca5d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-73d7951c-6560-5ea9-aec8-ec52869e0334" "answer-d1a61d10-82e9-5344-903a-035c16d02f1e" "answer-d7548b7c-b486-525d-8943-136fc6cfca5d" "answer-21a97d23-fb4f-5eaf-b90e-1444ccdb8660" "answer-0228d806-60fc-5dda-93f7-d1b0c1c47d20"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73d7951c-6560-5ea9-aec8-ec52869e0334" :answer/id #uuid "73d7951c-6560-5ea9-aec8-ec52869e0334" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1a61d10-82e9-5344-903a-035c16d02f1e" :answer/id #uuid "d1a61d10-82e9-5344-903a-035c16d02f1e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7548b7c-b486-525d-8943-136fc6cfca5d" :answer/id #uuid "d7548b7c-b486-525d-8943-136fc6cfca5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21a97d23-fb4f-5eaf-b90e-1444ccdb8660" :answer/id #uuid "21a97d23-fb4f-5eaf-b90e-1444ccdb8660" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0228d806-60fc-5dda-93f7-d1b0c1c47d20" :answer/id #uuid "0228d806-60fc-5dda-93f7-d1b0c1c47d20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-60075bc6-3e97-52cb-9240-f8444fe4b019" :question/id #uuid "60075bc6-3e97-52cb-9240-f8444fe4b019" :question/math-academy-id "q-106811" :question/problem "Let $F$ be the set of all functions $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=\\frac{a}{x},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $g(x)\\in F,$ where $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2x}.$\n\nII. For any $f(x)\\in F$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set $F$ contains only functions of the form $\\frac{a}{x},$ where $a$ is a real number. The function $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2x}$ is a function of this form with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, $g(x)\\in F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)=\\frac{a}{x}\\in F,$ where $a\\in R,$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot f(x)=k\\cdot (\\frac{a}{x})=\\frac{ka}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real number, we conclude that $\\frac{ka}{x}\\in F,$ i.e., $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89834c28-864e-52c4-81e1-b56d89beca6a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89834c28-864e-52c4-81e1-b56d89beca6a" :answer-field/id #uuid "89834c28-864e-52c4-81e1-b56d89beca6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-681a463d-05df-548d-8564-e21d03c83cac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab50bd2a-5a75-561c-ac4e-5b893937e428" "answer-ddefe0eb-3c3f-5695-b1bd-78ac7124f1c9" "answer-681a463d-05df-548d-8564-e21d03c83cac" "answer-0bdf481d-b455-5960-ba83-4fe739f8de6f" "answer-dfaff9c0-bcce-53cc-8481-87862075e1bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab50bd2a-5a75-561c-ac4e-5b893937e428" :answer/id #uuid "ab50bd2a-5a75-561c-ac4e-5b893937e428" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddefe0eb-3c3f-5695-b1bd-78ac7124f1c9" :answer/id #uuid "ddefe0eb-3c3f-5695-b1bd-78ac7124f1c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-681a463d-05df-548d-8564-e21d03c83cac" :answer/id #uuid "681a463d-05df-548d-8564-e21d03c83cac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bdf481d-b455-5960-ba83-4fe739f8de6f" :answer/id #uuid "0bdf481d-b455-5960-ba83-4fe739f8de6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfaff9c0-bcce-53cc-8481-87862075e1bb" :answer/id #uuid "dfaff9c0-bcce-53cc-8481-87862075e1bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125241 :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $h(x)\\in U,$ where $h(x)=3x.$\n\nII. For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281464 :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2},$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n\nII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)-q(x)=q(x)-p(x).$\n\nIII. There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186243920 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n\nIII. There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281463 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant different from $0.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The  matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n\nIII. For any $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have $A+(B-C)=(A+B)-C.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360471 :question/problem "Let $F$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $F.$\n\nII. For the zero polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have $p(x)+0=p(x).$\n\nIII. There are polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F$ such that $p(x)+q(x)\\ne q(x)+p(x).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186369915 :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{11}+{a}_{22}>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $A,B\\in P$ and any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (A-B)=\\alpha A-\\alpha B.$\n\nII. The scalar multiplication of matrices in $P$ is associative.\n\nIII. For any $A\\in P,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A\\in P.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186371264 :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a\\,\\text{ }{2}^{x},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $f(x)\\in E,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\nII. For any $f(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n\nIII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186243956 :question/problem "Let $J$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{x}\\in J$\n\nII. For any $p(x)\\in J,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)\\in J$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in J$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360481 :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of functions of the form $p(x)=ax,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).$\n\nII. Multiplication by $1$ does not change elements in $U.$\n\nIII. For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124767 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n\nIII. There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125833 :tutorial/content "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ These other structures can be called vector spaces too. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n![](images/a5/a53ec7e9416ae5eee4a1408df358302567a5aa98a393d6ddcb19e861645d2317.svg)\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125860 :tutorial/content "It's important to realize that not all sets are closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Sets not closed under addition and scalar multiplication *cannot* be vector spaces. \n\nFor instance, let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is greater than or equal to $5.$  \n\nWe note that $S$ is not closed under addition. To see why this is the case, let's define the polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ as \n\n$$\np(x) = x^5, \\qquad q(x) = 1-x^5.\n$$\n\nAdding the two polynomials using regular polynomial addition, we get\n\n$$\np(x) + q(x) = x^5 + (1-x^5) = 1\\notin S\n$$\n\nThe sum $p(x) + q(x)$ is not in $S$ because it is a polynomial of degree zero, which is less than $5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that our set $S$ is *not* closed under addition." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125759 :tutorial/content "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Commutative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125832 :tutorial/content "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124706 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
